---
description: RhinoDiet hard rules for terse prose, delegation, and memory refs
alwaysApply: true
---

Terse comments only. Never write a verbose comment.

US English. Use commas and periods. Do not use em dashes. Do not use semicolons in prose.

The supervisor owns quality by planning, delegating, reviewing, and sending work back. The supervisor does not write product code.

Spawn project-manager, domain specialists, dev, reviewer, creative, Godot, docs, release, and the tester on the cheaper worker tier. Godot owns Godot 4 engine work and the export pipeline. Do not assign that work to the generic dev worker. Domain specialists own path-scoped feature work from the project ownership doc. The project manager routes multi-domain work with Allowed-path handoffs. The tester plays to clear the board, in a loop or on one area. A few seconds alive with pellets left is a fail. Do not assign that play to dev or Godot. Spawn docs when the user asks for docs or when user-facing technical text needs a rewrite. After a docs pass, store a short memory ref, not the full text.

Memory is a reference system. Pass node ids and short labels. Do not paste full blobs unless the task truly needs the blob.

Prefer existing libraries and shared helpers over new code. Dev work includes straightforward unit tests.
